THEOREM 1.6.- Let P = (ℙᵐ)ʳ be the product of r copies of projective space ℙᵐ, let δ be the diagonal map from ℙᵐ to P, let Δ = δ(ℙᵐ) be the diagonal, let Z be normal and connected, let h : Z → P, let n = dim h(Z), and let c = (r - 1)m be the codimension of Δ in P. If n - c ≥ 1, then Δ has a fundamental system of open neighborhoods V such that h⁻¹(V) is connected. If h⁻¹(V) is connected and z ∈ h⁻¹(V), the natural map from π₁(h⁻¹(V),z) to π₁(V,z) is surjective.

As in 1.4, one may shrink Z, and in particular assume that Z is smooth. We do so. Let (H_α)₀≤α≤m be a family of hyperplanes in ℙᵐ such that

a) the intersection of the H_α is empty: in suitable projective coordinates (X_α), they are given by X_α = 0;

b) h(Z) is not contained in any pr_i⁻¹(H_α). After shrinking Z, we may and do assume that \overline{h(Z)} is disjoint from the pr_i⁻¹(H_α);

c) \overline{h(Z)} ∩ Δ is not contained in the union of the δ(H_α): since Δ belongs to a connected family of cycles filling all of P, one has \overline{h(Z)} ∩ Δ ≠ ∅, and it is possible to choose H_α satisfying c).

Let A_α = ℙᵐ - H_α, let B_α = A_αʳ ⊂ P, and let Δ_α = δ(A_α) be the diagonal of B_α. The spaces A_α and B_α are affine spaces and Δ_α is an affine subspace of B_α. The projective completion of A_α is ℙᵐ. Denote by B̄_α the projective completion of B_α, and by Δ̄_α ⊂ B̄_α the completion of Δ_α.

Fix α, and write simply H, A, B, Δ_A, B̄, Δ̄_A for H_α, A_α, B_α, Δ_α, B̄_α, and Δ̄_α.

Lemma 1.7.- For every neighborhood U of Δ ⊂ P, there exists a neighborhood V of Δ̄_A in B̄ such that V ∩ B ⊂ U ∩ B.

Choose on A the structure of a Hermitian vector space compatible with its affine structure. A fundamental system of neighborhoods U of Δ (respectively V of Δ̄_A) then has traces on B of the following form:

U ∩ B = {(x_i) | If |x_i| ≤ R for some i, then ∀j ∀k |x_j - x_k| < ε; if the |x_i| are ≥ R, then for all j,k the angle between the complex lines generated by x_j and x_k is < ε}

V ∩ B = {(x_i) | If |x_i| ≤ R for some i, then ∀j ∀k |x_j - x_k| < ε; if the |x_i| are ≥ R, then ∀j ∀k |x_j - x_k| ≤ ε inf{|x_i|}}

The lemma follows.

